WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.152
Recording the demo.

2
00:00:01.432 --> 00:00:03.843
The capstone demo is 5 to 8 minutes.

3
00:00:04.123 --> 00:00:10.288
That limit is deliberate: long enough to show one real task end to end, short enough that you must choose.

4
00:00:10.568 --> 00:00:13.277
The project page has the full script outline.

5
00:00:13.557 --> 00:00:15.477
Four principles sit behind it.

6
00:00:15.757 --> 00:00:17.208
Demo from the eval set.

7
00:00:17.488 --> 00:00:23.931
Choose the case you show from your eval cases (ideally at random from a slice you care about), and say so.

8
00:00:24.211 --> 00:00:30.931
A hand-picked showcase proves that one example works; a case from the eval set connects the demo to the numbers.

9
00:00:31.211 --> 00:00:32.192
Show a failure.

10
00:00:32.472 --> 00:00:37.037
Include an abstention or an error, and quote its frequency from the eval table.

11
00:00:37.317 --> 00:00:38.405
It feels risky.

12
00:00:38.685 --> 00:00:49.011
It is the single most credibility-building minute in the video, because every experienced reviewer knows the system fails sometimes and is waiting to see whether you know how often.

13
00:00:49.291 --> 00:00:51.339
Show the checks, not just the output.

14
00:00:51.619 --> 00:01:00.344
Narrate the decision points: why the agent called this tool, how the citation was verified, what the guardrail did with the injected instruction.

15
00:01:00.624 --> 00:01:03.888
Open one trace and read its cost and latency aloud.

16
00:01:04.168 --> 00:01:05.533
Make it reproducible.

17
00:01:05.813 --> 00:01:14.347
Write a script, rehearse once, and record in a fresh environment (a new container or a clean checkout) so nothing depends on state you forgot.

18
00:01:14.627 --> 00:01:15.715
Add captions.

19
00:01:15.995 --> 00:01:22.331
Don’t edit out slow steps without saying you did; if a step takes 20 seconds in reality, the viewer should know.
